;; perform-does-not-typecheck-its-arguments: the scalar-into-pointer check now
;; reaches a VARIABLE, not only a literal.
;;
;; The first pass at this report could only check literals, because an
;; un-annotated lambda parameter defaults to `int` and routinely carries a
;; `cstr` through it -- deliberately, since the bidirectional inference that
;; would refine it is gated OFF for primitive expected types to avoid codegen
;; churn.  So a TY_INT argument was ambiguous: a declared integer, or one
;; untyped carrier word.  Running the check in a LATER pass does not help; the
;; type is still `int` at effect_check time, because the carrier IS the
;; representation, not a not-yet-refined guess.
;;
;; What closed it was recording the missing information rather than guessing at
;; it: `Binding.type_is_carrier_default` marks a parameter whose type the author
;; did not write, set where the default is taken and cleared by an annotation
;; (and by the bidirectional inference, when that fires).  A declared `int`
;; below is now distinguishable from a carrier `int`, so this is reported while
;; `tests/fixtures/effect-type-alias` and `cps-backend-fn-param-effectful` --
;; the carrier shape, and this check's controls -- still compile and run.
;;
;; An un-annotated `let` bound directly to such a parameter inherits the
;; provenance, so `(let [m msg] (perform (Log m)))` is declined one indirection
;; along too.
(defeffect Log [msg : cstr] : int)

(defn w [n : int] : int (perform (Log n)))

(defn main [] : int
  (println (handle (w 42) (Log [m] k) (do (println m) (resume k 0))))
  0)
